@startuml "Bridge - Files (Problem) - Class Diagram"
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
title Before — a client's document store, three ways it could have been written


package "1 - one class, a switch on each axis" {
  class SwitchingFileManager {
    + Save(department, store, path, content) : string
    - SaveForFinance(store, ..) : string
    - SaveForInsurance(store, ..) : string
  }
  note bottom of SwitchingFileManager
    2 departments x 3 stores = <b>6 branches</b>, by hand.
    Six of them store something; <b>five</b> then trim.

    The insurance-on-FileNet branch has no retention
    loop at all, so insurance keeps eight versions
    where it is allowed two. Nothing throws. The
    vendor is happy. Only an auditor finds it — or
    the test that counts what was kept.
  end note
}

package "2 - a class per (department, store) pair" {
  class FinanceEvernoteManager {
    - Keep = 5
  }
  class InsuranceEvernoteManager {
    - Keep = 2
  }
  class FinanceSharePointManager {
    - Keep = 5
  }
  class "...three more..." as More
  note bottom of More
    The class name has to state <b>both axes</b>,
    which is the smell: a name carrying two
    ideas is hiding a field.

    2 x 3 = <b>6 classes</b>. Same store, two
    departments: the vendor calls are duplicated.
    Same department, two stores: the retention
    number is.
  end note
}

package "3 - the store becomes the base class" {
  abstract class EvernoteStore {
    # Put(path, content) : string
    # KeepOnly(path, versions) : void
  }
  class EvernoteBoundFinanceManager {
    - Keep = 5
    + Save(path, content) : string
  }
  EvernoteStore <|-- EvernoteBoundFinanceManager
  note bottom of EvernoteBoundFinanceManager
    A <b>real improvement</b>: the Evernote calls are
    written once now instead of once per department.

    And still welded. There is no SetStore here and
    there cannot be, so when the Evernote contract
    ends this object cannot follow the documents.
    A test asserts no such method exists.
  end note
}

' Graphviz places packages by content, not by declaration order. Hidden edges pin
' them to the order the deck teaches them in.
SwitchingFileManager -[hidden]right- FinanceEvernoteManager
FinanceSharePointManager -[hidden]right- EvernoteStore

note as N1
  All three work, and the first one has been in production.
  <b>The two axes are welded together</b> in each: who owns the
  document, and which vendor holds the bytes. Nothing about
  either list is derived from the other, which is exactly why
  they multiply.
end note

@enduml
